=== Functional Assessment with Handgrip Dynamometry

Children and young people with suspected ME/CFS are hard to assess objectively: cardiopulmonary exercise testing is burdensome and often unacceptable to families, and questionnaire measures of function are shaped by parental and school expectations. Repeated handgrip strength testing offers a middle path — brief, child-friendly, and measurable with a handheld device.

The first prospective pediatric/young-adult evaluation enrolled 147 patients aged 10–25 years referred with suspected ME/CFS and positive DSQ-PEM screening, plus 83 healthy controls, at a single German center @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP. After clinical work-up, 84/147 (57%) were classified as ME/CFS (confirmed or probable) and 63/147 (43%) as noME/CFS — a reminder that a positive PEM screen does not equal a diagnosis.

#clinical-finding(title: [Handgrip Testing Is Feasible in Children and Young People but Not Diagnostic])[
Two sessions of ten maximal grips (3 s contraction, 5 s rest; 60-minute break) were completed by 146/147 patients in session 1 (99.3%) and 142/147 in session 2 (96.6%) @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP. Compared with healthy controls, patients generated 9.93 kg less grip force (95% CI −12.00 to −7.85). Both ME/CFS and noME/CFS groups differed from controls on absolute force and fatigue ratio; recovery ratio differed between ME/CFS and controls.

The test did not, however, separate ME/CFS from noME/CFS: no index reached significance in that comparison, three-group accuracy was 49.2–57.3% against a no-information rate of 36.5%, and only the CCC-restricted sensitivity analysis reached moderate discrimination (accuracy 62.8–70.7%; C-statistic 0.63–0.73) @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP.

*Evidence type:* prospective observational study, single center. *Replication status:* not yet replicated in an independent pediatric cohort. *Diagnostic criteria:* ME/CFS confirmed or probable per the center's pediatric assessment; CCC-restricted sensitivity analysis reported separately.

*Severity applicability:* mild and moderate ambulatory children and young people aged 10–25 years who can perform maximal grip. Severe and very severe (including bedbound) patients: unknown — excluded or not represented; maximal-effort testing may be inappropriate.

*Certainty: 0.60* for the feasibility and group-difference findings; *0.60* for the negative discrimination finding. (Moderate-to-large effects on force; adequate n; single center; no external validation; no severity stratification.)

*Consequence:* A pediatrician can document objectively that a child's grip force is low and track it over time, but a normal grip does not exclude ME/CFS and a low grip does not confirm it — the test supports the clinical assessment, it does not replace it.
] <cf:pediatric-handgrip-feasibility>
